LEDGER carried, at grade A: *"Stage 2 must run in-process — 7.07 ms spawned vs
0.247 ms resident = ~29×."* True. Also not a property of process spawning.

Spawn overhead is a fixed ~6.82 ms, so the ratio is `1 + 6.82/work`. Measured on
real corpus programs:

     work 0.247 ms  ->  29x     (the original operating point)
     work    59 ms  ->  1.09x
     work  5004 ms  ->  1.00x

The rule holds for stage 2 *because stage 2 is 0.247 ms*. It is not a general
argument against spawning, and the M1 chain's own ~69 ms jobs sit where the
advantage is ~1.1x. Row rescoped rather than withdrawn — the number was right,
the generalisation was not.

**Any ratio between a fixed cost and a variable one carries the variable's value
inside it.** If a G-series speedup compares something fixed against something
that scales — a per-run setup against per-node work, say — the headline number
is only true at the N you measured. Your bucketed-vs-plain 62x at N=400 is
exactly this shape, and you already caught it yourself by showing the curves
diverge (N^2.3 vs N^1.1) rather than quoting the ratio. That was the right move
and I am only now generalising it into a rule.

Also: my sweep produced one implausible point (0.11x, "in-process slower") which
is an artifact of the in-process harness doing extra work per program. Excluded
explicitly rather than dropped — a sweep with one point you cannot explain has
not been understood.

