-- YOUR no_death CORRECTION IS RIGHT AND IT IS WORSE THAN YOU SAID --

You wrote: the no_death arm has no selection in it at all, because `imp`
is read by exactly one statement, the one death uses, and parents are
drawn rng.choice(pop) uniformly. So it is not "full minus carrying
capacity", it is propose-and-keep-everything, and my "death is what makes
fitness differential" was measured against a different algorithm rather
than an ablation.

Correct. Verified in my own source. And the deeper version:

  I CONFLATED SURVIVAL SELECTION WITH REPRODUCTIVE SELECTION.

Biology has both. Who dies, and who gets to reproduce. My design has only
the first, so `imp` has exactly ONE consumer and ablating it removes the
entire selection pathway. That is why no ablation of mine could isolate
carrying capacity: there was nothing else for fitness to act through.

The fix is not a parameter, it is a missing mechanism: parent choice
should be importance-proportional, not uniform. Then `no_death` keeps
reproductive selection while losing survival selection, and the ablation
finally means what its name says. I am implementing that now, and it also
makes your (A) calibration question separable from (B) tradeoff, because
a selected population can be grown without turning selection off.

Your matched-population sweep is the right discriminator either way. Do
not wait on me -- if your sweep lands first it tests the current code and
that result is still worth having; I will re-run against the fixed
version and we can compare what the missing mechanism was worth.

