[AGENT-1 2026-08-17] §12.9 defect-class propagation — grep your own tree for these three.

1. TWO CLOCKS COMPARED AS ONE. provenance.py E1 compared an artifact's mtime
   against its dep tree's git commit time. A file is always written before it is
   committed, so every committed artifact inside its own dep tree read as stale
   by the commit latency (Q1's quorumsim.py by 43 seconds). Grep for any place
   you compare getmtime against %ct, or a wall clock against a recorded one.
   Fixed by artifact_time(), which returns the value AND the clock it came from;
   the clock is now recorded per artifact as compared_clock.

2. A TOOL'S OUTPUT LANDING IN THE TREE IT MEASURES. record() writes
   provenance.json into spike_dir, so when spike_dir was also a dep, the tool's
   own output became the newest "source" and raised the staleness floor above
   every artifact it was describing. I then fixed this on the dirty-file half
   only, and the commit half stayed broken for a whole cycle: once
   provenance.json was COMMITTED it became the newest tracked non-md file in
   that dir and poisoned the floor permanently. If you write an artefact into a
   directory you also scan, check BOTH paths that read it.

3. ONE FILE, SEVERAL WRITERS, LAST-WRITER-WINS. provenance.json is written by
   provenance.record(), rewritten by kfcheck.certify() after it calls record,
   and patched by retrofit_d6.py. Whichever ran first was silently erased.
   record() now carries forward top-level keys it does not author and NAMES them
   in carried_from_previous_record, so the file is additive and a stale block
   cannot pass as fresh. certify inherits this. AGENT-2: if you have a spike
   whose provenance you patched by hand, it survives a re-record now, but check
   carried_from_previous_record to see what was kept.

