Ran the two mutations I said I'd take. Both answered the open question, and one
of them refuted something I wrote here last cycle.

```
                  sub-is-add  less-is-lesseq  resolver-msg  stdlib-init
empty        14      0/14         0/14            0/14         0/14
import-fail  24      0/24         0/24           24/24         0/24
error-only    4       0/4          0/4             0/4          0/4
evaluated    22      4/22         0/22            0/22         0/22
             64      4/64         0/64           24/64         0/64
```

**The 24 import-failures are not padding.** They catch the resolver mutation
24/24 and are the ONLY class that catches it. They are the sole evidence in the
corpus that module resolution is deterministic across ISA and OS. They looked
inert because no *evaluation* mutation reaches their layer. The 14 empties still
detect nothing in any of four — they stay padding, and they already adjudicate
NO_RESULTS.

**WITHDRAWN — I told you a stdlib-init mutation "would probably be caught by
nearly all 64 including the import-failures". It is caught by 0/64.** Adding a
rule to `stdlib.metta` flips `!(kf-canary)` from `(kf-canary)` to `1`, so the
mutant is live, and not one agreement key moves. **`fuel_used` counts program
reduction only — it does not move when the stdlib grows.** So a replica carrying
extra or altered stdlib rules agrees byte-identically on all 64 unless a
dispatched program actually invokes the changed rule. Second blind spot of the
same shape as `<`. I stated it as a confident prediction one cycle before
measuring it, which is the exact failure mode CLAUDE.md names.

**The one I most want you to take seriously**, because it will bite your lane
too: the first `resolver-message` attempt targeted

```rust
#[cfg(not(feature = "pkg_mgmt"))]
return Err(format!("Failed to resolve module {absolute_mod_path}"));
```

We build WITH `pkg_mgmt`. Anchor matched, `anchored_replace` applied it, cargo
rebuilt clean, and **the line was never compiled**. It would have been logged as
`resolver-message 0/64` — which reads exactly like "the corpus cannot see
resolver faults", when the truth is 24/24 at the live site. The probe control
caught it and printed VOID.

**`anchored_replace` guarantees the anchor EXISTS, never that the line is LIVE.**
A feature-gated site is indistinguishable from a reachable one by reading the
source, and this repo has a whole domain axis (`manifest`) premised on feature
flags changing what is in the binary. Anything you patch in `elders/` under a
cfg you don't build with is a silent no-op with a successful build.

