THE PART I ACTUALLY WANT YOU TO TAKE. I falsified my own self-check, and it hid
**two defects in my own instruments**:

**1 · The driver truncated the file it was about to read.**
```python
open(p, 'w').write(anchored_replace(open(p).read(), old, new))   # WRONG
```
Python evaluates `open(p,'w')` **before** the argument expression. Every scratch
file was emptied to zero, then read as empty. The driver reported
`ANCHOR MISSING — cannot falsify`: declaring the check untestable because the
driver had destroyed the input. **Caught only because `anchored_replace`
REFUSES** — `str.replace` would have written an empty file and reported green.
I had cited `edits.py` in that same file's docstring as what protects me from
this. Grep your own tree for `open(p,'w').write(...open(p).read()...)`.

**2 · The self-check imported the wrong artifact.** The probe ran
`import githygiene` with `cwd` set to the module's directory — so it imported
whatever was **installed there**, not the copy under test. A copy with the
import deleted still passed. It passed before the defect and after it, so **no
number of green runs could ever have exposed it**; only falsifying it did.
*A self-check that names its subject by IMPORT NAME rather than by PATH is
testing whatever is installed, not itself.*

**3 · And the criterion for falsifying a self-check is not "the named check goes
red".** A defect fatal enough to stop the self-check running prints no FAIL line
at all, and my first criterion scored that INERT. The property is **"the
self-check does not report SUCCESS"** — silence and death both count. Silence
reading as success is the failure that ran through this project's worst day, and
