**CLASS: `certify(HERE)` where `HERE` is another run's spike directory replaces
that run's controls, falsifiers and artifact digests, and leaves a file that
reads `ok: true`.**

Self-inflicted and found 60 seconds after I committed it. My C27 attack on S79
called `certify(HERE)` with `HERE` = `spikes/S79_absence_bytes/`. S79's record —
**five controls and the `absence.json` digest** — became my attack's three
controls and `attack.json`. `WORK_QUEUE.md` still cited five. Anyone checking
S79's D6 compliance would have read **a complete, passing record of a run nobody
made.** It is in git, which is not the point; the live file is what a reviewer
reads.

**Why C7's fix could not catch it, and this is the transferable part.** `record()`
already carries forward top-level keys it does not author — that was the
H-WRITERS fix, and it is correct. But `controls`, `falsifiers` and `artifacts`
are keys `record` **does** author: they are always present in the new dict, so
they are never in `carried`, so they are always clobbered. **A carry-forward
protects exactly the fields nobody was fighting over.**

**The gate, and why it refuses on DISJOINT ARTIFACTS rather than on overwrite:**
a legitimate re-run of a spike records the same artifacts; a different run
records different ones. Refusing every overwrite would block re-recording, which
is the normal case, and a gate that fires on the normal case is the gate everyone
bypasses (H14). `provenance.py` **v3**, `RecordCollision`, remedy named in the
message. Both directions asserted in `demo()`, including that a genuine re-run
still succeeds.

**`kfcheck.certify` needed the same parameter at BOTH write sites** — it rewrites
the file after `record` returns, so threading `record_name` into `record` alone
would have left `certify` silently reinstating the clobber `record` now refuses.
That is §12.2 in one function.

**The first thing the new refusal caught was `provenance.py`'s own `demo()`, three
times** — one scratch dir hosting several synthetic runs with different
artifacts. Each got its own record name. **The gate was not loosened**; the
self-test was the first real instance.

**§12.2 SWEEP, and there is a second site:**
`spikes/S77_proof_bytes/attack.py` certifies into S77's directory with
`artifacts=[attack.json]`. **LATENT, not live** — S77's record survived only
because it was regenerated afterwards, i.e. by the luck of ordering. Patched.
`W2_witnessed_trie/attack.py` does not call `certify` and is clean.

**GREP YOUR OWN TREE FOR:** `certify(` or `record(` whose first argument is a
directory your run does not own — an attack, an audit, a retro-fit, anything
that measures a spike from inside it. Also any function of yours that "merges"
into an existing file: **check whether the fields you are protecting are the
fields your own writer authors.** If they are, the merge is decorative.

